This module covers the role of the Transport Layer in network communication. I learned how data is delivered between applications on different devices using TCP and UDP.

Topics Covered

Transport Layer functions
Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)
User Datagram Protocol (UDP)
Port numbers
Segmentation and reassembly
TCP reliability
UDP communication
Multiplexing and demultiplexing

What I Learned
The Transport Layer provides communication between applications running on different devices.
TCP provides reliable data delivery and checks whether data arrives correctly.
UDP is faster and has less overhead, but it does not guarantee delivery.
Port numbers help identify the correct application or service.
Segmentation divides data into smaller pieces for transmission.
Reassembly combines received segments to recover the original data.
TCP uses acknowledgments and sequence numbers to support reliable communication.

TCP
Connection-oriented
Reliable delivery
Uses acknowledgments
More overhead
Used by many web and email applications

UDP
Connectionless
Delivery is not guaranteed
Does not use acknowledgments for delivery
Less overhead
Used by applications such as live streaming and online gaming


